course: everyday
lesson: 485
title: What a real friend is
level: primary-4
stars: 3
explain: Sam's dad asks, «So what makes a real friend?» Sam thinks of Jake turning up with Monster Munch for a rematch, Tom swapping sleeping bags for Kieran, Mr Grimshaw with his tomatoes. He writes a list: turns up, keeps secrets, says sorry and means it, is the same in front of everyone. He sticks it inside his wardrobe door. A real friend is shown by what they do, not by what they say.
explain: Knowing who isn't a friend matters too. Lily lists the people who make her feel small: Grace, Bella, queen_slay. «I don't need any of you.» She still says hello to Grace and Bella, but stops trying to please them. Kieran still doesn't like Jake, but he's seen him stand up for Sam and give back a ball that wasn't his: «He's alright, I s'pose. Still a wasteman.» They nod in the corridor, and that's enough. You don't have to be friends to treat someone with respect.
explain: Some lessons were learned the hard way. Connor plays online every Friday now, but only with school friends and his cousin, chat set to friends only; ShadowWolf feels like a lifetime ago. Mia remembers her first day in Ohio, pretending to read the fire notice by the wall; now, when a new girl comes, she walks straight over: «Hey, wanna play four square? We need one more.» The new girl's face lights up exactly like Mia's did. And two years late, Jake catches Sam at the bike shed: «That time I told the team you were scared of the dark. That was proper out of order. Sorry, mate.» «Yeah, it was. Cheers, though.» They ride home together.
explain: Real friendships take many shapes. Maddie in Perth and Lily at home are still best friends a year on; their calls are shorter, but Maddie still says «beanbag day» on the anniversary and Lily still eats a biscuit for it. Mum and Auntie Jo laugh till they cry about something from 1998: thirty years of Fridays. Lily texts Maddie, «we have to be like Mum and Jo». «obvs. bingo ladies.» Sam's dog Scout waits at the door at 3.40 every day, tail going like a windscreen wiper on full. Tank the bulldog still growls at Sam every morning, and Sam still says, «Alright, Tank.» And on Sam's birthday, Mr Grimshaw, once «Grumpy Grimshaw», leaves a tomato plant on the step: «Water daily. No footballs near it.»
fact: A real friend turns up when it matters, keeps your secrets, says sorry and means it, and is the same in public and in private.
fact: Friendship is shown by what people do, more than by what they say.
fact: Frenemies and people who make you feel small can be let go; being polite to them is enough.
fact: You can respect someone without being their friend.
fact: Pets can be real friends, and they need looking after in return.
fact: Remembering how it felt to be new helps you be the one who invites someone in.
fact: Playing online only with people you know in real life, with friends-only settings, keeps you safe.
fact: A proper sorry names what you did, and even a late one can heal.
fact: Distance doesn't end a real friendship; small rituals and regular calls keep it alive.
fact: Friendships can last decades when both people keep turning up, through fall-outs and moves.
fact: An enemy can become a friend, sometimes because of one small kind act.
fact: Respecting an animal's space, like a growly dog's, is a kind of friendship too.
fact: «S'pose» means suppose, «obvs» means obviously, and «cheers» can mean thanks.
exercise: What was on Sam's list of what makes a real friend? | Turns up, keeps secrets, says sorry, same in front of everyone | Has the best games and toys, shares them, and never says no to you | Lives next door and is the same age | Always agrees with you
exercise: What did Lily realise about Grace, Bella and queen_slay? | She didn't need people who made her feel small | She had to win them over, whatever it took | They were secretly her best friends all along | She should be mean back
exercise: How does Lily treat Grace and Bella now? | Says hello but stops trying to please them | Ignores them completely and rudely | Tries even harder to please them every day at school | Spreads rumours about them
exercise: What is Kieran's view of Jake now? | Not a friend, but someone he respects with a nod | His best friend forever and ever, no matter what | His worst enemy he still fights | Someone he's never met
exercise: Why does Kieran respect Jake a bit? | Jake stood up for Sam and gave back a ball | Jake gave him money for sweets at the shop | Jake let him win a race | Jake finally apologised to Kieran for everything
exercise: How does Connor play online now? | With school friends and his cousin, friends-only chat | With anyone at all who sends him gifts or skins | With ShadowWolf every night | He has stopped playing forever
exercise: What does Mia do when a new girl arrives? | Walks straight over and invites her to play | Waits a few days to see if she's cool enough | Ignores her like she was ignored | Tells everyone about her
exercise: Why does Mia invite new kids? | She remembers how it felt to be new | Her teacher pays her | She needs more people for her team only | She wants to boss them about
exercise: How do Maddie and Lily keep their friendship going across the world? | Calls and little rituals like «beanbag day» | They never speak but stay friends | They moved back next door to each other last year | They only send presents
exercise: How long have Mum and Auntie Jo been friends? | About thirty years | About three weeks | About three years | Since last Friday
exercise: What did Jake say at the bike shed two years later? | Sorry for telling the team Sam was scared of the dark | Sorry for beating Sam at Mario Kart | That Sam still owed him a bike for the one he broke | Nothing, he just rode off
exercise: How did Sam answer Jake's late sorry? | «Yeah, it was. Cheers, though» | «It doesn't matter, it never did» | «Too late, mate» | He ignored him
exercise: What does Scout do every day at 3.40? | Waits at the door, wagging hard | Growls at Sam | Sleeps in the garden | Runs away down the road to the park gate
exercise: How does Sam treat Tank the growly bulldog? | Says «Alright, Tank» and respects his space | Tries to pat him and hug him every day | Growls back at him | Avoids his whole street completely, every time
exercise: What did Mr Grimshaw leave for Sam's birthday? | A tomato plant with a note | A brand new football | A bag of toffees only | A bill for the broken greenhouse window
exercise: What does «obvs» mean? | Obviously | Obey | Oh boy | Of course not
test: «a real mate is someone who's got the best skins and buys you stuff». Right? | No, a real friend turns up and keeps secrets | Yes, gifts prove friendship | Yes, if the skins are rare and cost loads of money | No, real friends have no skins
test: «Grace is so mean but I gotta make her like me». What did Lily decide? | She didn't need her, polite was enough | She tried even harder to please her | She became just like Grace | She stopped talking to everyone
test: «If you don't like someone you have to be horrible to them». True? | No, you can respect someone without being friends | Yes, it's only fair to treat them the same way | Yes, so they know | No, you must be best friends instead
test: «ShadowWolf was my best mate, Dad ruined it». Looking back, what's true? | Dad kept Connor safe from a stranger | Dad was wrong to block him without asking | ShadowWolf was surely a kid his own age | Connor should find him again on a new account
test: «new kid's stood by the wall pretending to read the fire notice, lol». What would Mia do? | Go over and invite her to play | Laugh along with everyone | Wait for a teacher to sort it | Tell her the fire notice is boring
test: «Jake's sorry is two years late, that's mad, it doesn't count». Does it? | Yes, a proper late sorry can still heal | No, sorries have a time limit | No, only sorries said on the very same day count | Yes, but only with a present
test: «Maddie moved to Australia so they're not best friends any more». What's true? | Distance doesn't end a real friendship | Friends always forget each other after moving | Calls don't count as friendship | They need to live in one town
test: «Scout's just a dog, he's not a real friend». What does the lesson say? | Pets can be real friends who need care too | Dogs can't feel anything | Only humans can be friends | Pets only love food
test: What would probably happen if Sam kept trying to pat Tank, who growls every day? | He could get bitten | Tank would become his best friend | Tank would stop growling for good | Mr Doyle would give him Tank
test: «Grumpy Grimshaw gave Sam a plant, that's sus». What really changed him? | Small kindnesses over time turned an enemy into a friend | He was always secretly nice to everyone | Sam's dad paid him | He wanted Sam to give him back his football from the garden
test: In a film, the cool kid has hundreds of friends who all disappear when trouble comes. What does the lesson say a real friend does? | Turns up when it matters | Disappears when there's trouble | Only stays for the fun parts | Counts how many likes you get
test: «we have to be like Mum and Jo». «obvs. bingo ladies.» What are Lily and Maddie hoping for? | A friendship that lasts their whole lives | To play bingo every night when they are old | To fall out like Mum and Jo did and never make up | To move to the same town tomorrow and live next door
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises a true friend as defined in the lesson? | One who attends in need, keeps confidences, apologises sincerely and is consistent in public and private | One who supplies the most numerous and costly gifts and is admired by the greatest number of peers | One who agrees invariably with every opinion expressed, in public and in private | One who resides in closest proximity
exam: Ascertain the appropriate conduct towards persons who habitually diminish one's self-regard. | To remain courteous whilst ceasing to seek their approval | To redouble one's efforts to secure their approval | To retaliate by diminishing them in turn | To adopt their conduct as one's own
exam: Select the response consistent with the established facts concerning friendships maintained across great distances. | They may endure where regular contact and small customs are maintained | They necessarily terminate upon relocation | They are not genuine friendships | They require that both parties reside in one town and attend one school
exam: Identify the principal practice, as established in the lesson, which renders online play safe for a child. | Play restricted to persons known in life, with chat limited to friends | Play with any person who offers gifts | Disclosure of one's address to trusted players | The use of voice chat with strangers
